570379cc51602480cf3cb7a03eee83ed4ef8869950a893a439c850402f1","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-emphasis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The focused element is a **person**, and the following clause needs its subject.","**Who** links the focused person to the action in this it-cleft.","Choose **who**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Where does not represent the person performing this action.","B":"What does not normally follow a named person as the cleft connector here.","C":"**Who** links the focused person to the action in this it-cleft.","D":"Whose would need a noun after it to mark possession."}}},{"id":"c3d0bcf0-a8f9-59eb-aa35-51472388ac18","topicSlug":"clefts-emphasis","level":"C1","prompt":"Complete the what-cleft: “What I need ____ time to repair the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"am"},{"id":"B","text":"is"},{"id":"C","text":"are"},{"id":"D","text":"be"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The subject is the whole **what I need** clause, not the I inside it.","explanation":"The what-clause presents a single need, so **is** links it to time.","keywords":["what-cleft-agreement"],"skill":"what-cleft-agreement","contextKey":"clefts-emphasis-50","fingerprint":"6da691b3472c07d3f6673074cdd22b1be1c782621c6f3a1cbddb7dfdee1a4abc","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-emphasis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The subject is the whole **what I need** clause, not the I inside it.","The what-clause presents a single need, so **is** links it to time.","Choose **is**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Am agrees with I as a main subject, not with the whole what-clause.","B":"The what-clause presents a single need, so **is** links it to time.","C":"The complement is uncountable time and the clause presents one need, so plural are does not fit this example.","D":"Be is not a finite present form."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Focus the reader’s attention on a participant, an action or a correction.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"It-clefts","explanation":"An it-cleft divides a message into a focused part and a following clause: **It was X who/that ...**. The focus can be a person, thing, time or place. Use a form that makes the intended contrast clear.","pattern":"**It was the guide who** called.","commonMistakes":["It in an it-cleft is a grammatical device; it does not refer back to a thing."],"examples":["Correct the claim that she never does the task: “She does **prepare** the visitor guide!”","Correct the claim that she never does the task: “She does **restore** the old portrait!”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"What-clefts","explanation":"A what-clause can introduce the part of the message already under discussion, followed by be and the focus. What I need is a break focuses on a break. After what + do, the focused action commonly uses a base infinitive, with to also possible in many contexts.","pattern":"**What I need is** more time. / **What she did was (to) call**.","commonMistakes":["Do not reject the to variant after what she did was merely because a bare infinitive is also possible."],"examples":["Correct a denial about yesterday: “We did **prepare** the visitor guide!”","Correct a denial about yesterday: “We did **restore** the old portrait!”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Emphatic do","explanation":"Use do, does or did in an affirmative clause to contradict a negative assumption or add emphasis. The auxiliary carries tense and agreement, so the main verb uses its base form. Spoken stress falls on the auxiliary.","pattern":"She **does know**. / We **did call**.","commonMistakes":["She does knows marks agreement twice; use does know."],"examples":["Focus on the person: “It was the assistant **who** prepared the visitor guide.”","Focus on the person: “It was the assistant **who** restored the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Choose emphasis for a reason","explanation":"Clefts and emphatic auxiliaries make information prominent. Too many in a paragraph can make prose heavy. Choose the structure according to what the listener already knows and which contrast matters. Written emphasis should still leave the underlying grammar clear.","pattern":"It was **yesterday** that we called.","commonMistakes":["Clefts are not needed simply to make every sentence sound more advanced."],"examples":["Complete the what-cleft: “What I need **is** time to prepare the visitor guide.”","Complete the what-cleft: “What I need **is** time to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**It was the guide who** called.","**What I need is** more time. / **What she did was (to) call**.","She **does know**. / We **did call**.","It was **yesterday** that we called."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["inversion"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
